{"id": "DeBKeb8TLIA", "title": "Video by therishikatha", "description": "Why is Maa Parvati called Aparna? 🌿\nShe lived on fallen leaves for 3,000 years — and then gave up even those.\n\n▎\n\nPrincess Parvati wanted to marry Shiva, but the great yogi wouldn’t even open his eyes.\n\nSo she left behind her crown and silks, dressed in a saffron robe, took up a rudraksha mala and water pot, and began her tapasya.\n\nFor 1,000 years, she ate only fruits and flowers.\nFor 100 years, only leafy greens.\nFor 3,000 years, only fallen bilva leaves.\n\nThen she gave up even those.\n\nAnd so, the gods named her **Aparna — “she who gave up even a leaf.”** 🍃\n\n▎\n\nOne day, a stranger appeared and began mocking Shiva.\n\nParvati wouldn’t hear a word against him.\n\nBut the stranger was Shiva himself, testing her devotion.\n\nHe finally revealed himself and said, **“From today, I am yours, won by your tapasya.”**\n\n▎\n\nThis is the story of **Maa Brahmacharini**, worshipped on Day 2 of Navratri. 🪷\n\nHer story of determination is **Book 2 of our Navadurga picture-book series**, created for ages 4–8.\n\n📚 Get all 3 books for ₹600 at **rishikatha.com** (link in bio).\n\n▎\n\n💬 Bappa gave up his modak. What could *you* give up for one day?\n👀 Did you spot the two clues that the stranger was Shiva?\n📌 Save this for Navratri Day 2, and share it with someone who never gives up.\n\n#brahmacharini #maabrahmacharini #navratri #navratriday2 #navadurga aparna parvati shivparvati ganesha hindumythology indianmythology mythologyforkids picturebooks kidsbooks rishikatha", "channel": "therishikatha", "uploader_id": "22569163881", "uploader": "Rishi Katha", "timestamp": 1791000575, "like_count": 11589, "comment_count": 72, "http_headers": {"Referer": "https://www.instagram.com/"}, "formats": [{"format_id": "dash-1500668345200774a", "ext": "m4a", "tbr": 46.556, "asr": 44100, "format_note": "DASH audio", "container": "m4a_dash", "vcodec": "none", "acodec": "mp4a.40.5", "url": "https://instagram.f